## Return Early, Return Often

Sometimes you want to bail out of a function immediately if something is wrong. That's an **early return**.

```xiom
fn divide_safely(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
  if b == 0 {
    return 0;  // Exit early — can't divide by zero!
  }
  return a / b;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let result = divide_safely(10, 0);  // returns 0
  return result;
}
```

### The Guard Clause Pattern

Put your error checks at the top and return early. The rest of your function stays clean:

```xiom
fn calculate_score(raw: Int, bonus: Int) -> Int {
  // Guard: reject negative input
  if raw < 0 {
    return 0;
  }

  // Guard: cap bonus
  if bonus > 100 {
    return raw + 100;
  }

  // Main logic — no nesting needed!
  return raw + bonus;
}
```

### Without Early Return (Messy)

```xiom
fn calculate_score(raw: Int, bonus: Int) -> Int {
  if raw < 0 {
    return 0;
  } else {
    if bonus > 100 {
      return raw + 100;
    } else {
      return raw + bonus;
    }
  }
}
```

Early returns flatten the pyramid of doom!

### Multiple Early Returns

You can have as many early returns as you need:

```xiom
fn get_grade(score: Int) -> Str {
  if score > 100 { return "Invalid"; }
  if score >= 90  { return "A"; }
  if score >= 80  { return "B"; }
  if score >= 70  { return "C"; }
  if score >= 60  { return "D"; }
  return "F";
}
```

### return Means Stop

When you hit `return`, the function stops IMMEDIATELY. Nothing after it in that branch runs.

syntax_ref:
fn name(params) -> Type {
  if condition {
    return value;  // exits function immediately
  }
  // only reached if condition is false
  return other_value;
}

